WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:07.410
Hey, David, are landslide elections even possible
in the modern United States?

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.280
So there's a couple different considerations
here.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:19.560
Understand that with the Electoral College
system, you can have a close popular vote,

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.810
relatively, and a huge Electoral College victory.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.310
It can happen.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:31.019
You can also have a popular vote win for one
candidate while they actually lose the Electoral

00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:32.019
College.

00:00:32.019 --> 00:00:35.510
As we have seen help Republicans multiple
times in the last couple of decades, including

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:39.030
most most recently, Donald Trump in 2016.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:47.120
The reason in our country that you are less
likely to see these overwhelming landslide

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.780
victories right now, it's less about the system.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:55.210
The system very much can allow an overwhelming
Electoral College victory.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.810
It's more about polarization.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.420
The entire country agrees on almost nothing.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.880
I imagined when the corona virus was starting,
this isn't how naive was.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:06.880
I was.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.829
What an idiot, right?

00:01:08.829 --> 00:01:11.899
We're not going to politicize a virus.

00:01:11.899 --> 00:01:15.060
We're going to come together as Americans
and we're going to do what needs to be done.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.979
And we're going to keep people safe and we're
going to speak logically and it's going to

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:20.679
be connected to the facts on the ground.

00:01:20.679 --> 00:01:22.799
Boy, was I wrong.

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:29.759
And if the country can't even agree barely
about something like a virus so empirical,

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:37.819
so fact based, then it is very difficult to
imagine that you could have such lopsidedness

00:01:37.819 --> 00:01:42.110
in the United States that you would see such
an overwhelming landslide.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:47.889
Now, that being said, how we define landslide
is up to us.

00:01:47.889 --> 00:01:54.179
And you could see this November what in the
modern era would be considered a landslide

00:01:54.179 --> 00:01:57.969
victory for Joe Biden or he might lose or
he might lose.

00:01:57.969 --> 00:02:04.459
Now, the bigger picture is that you have a
lot of people in this country and this gets

00:02:04.459 --> 00:02:05.459
to critical thinking.

00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:08.810
This gets the education that this gets the
media literacy.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:11.970
You have a lot of people in this country who
will never vote for a Democratic candidate.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.310
You also have a lot of people in this country
who will never vote for a Republican candidate,

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:20.660
regardless of their platform platforms and
regardless of what they actually stand for.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:28.030
There are then a bunch of people in the middle
who will see reason, but it is tough.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:33.670
It really requires fixing and changing a lot
of the systems that keep people in these filter

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:39.910
bubbles and echo chambers and such when we
look at how they are distributed around the

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:40.910
country.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:44.860
That's where the potential for a landslide
really comes when you look at some of the

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:46.820
polling for the upcoming election.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:52.430
And you see, wow. Trump is only up two in
a recent Texas poll from the Dallas Morning

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:53.430
News.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:03:01.670
Wow. Trump is only winning in Missouri by
five, whereas he won in 2016 by 19.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:06.140
Demographic changes, older voters dying, quite
frankly.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:12.190
And immigration from Latin America coming
in could actually create a situation where,

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:15.940
you know, we've been seeing it for a while,
but it could be in four years and eight years,

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:16.940
in 12 years.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.290
Texas could be a purple state that sometimes
votes for a Democrat.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:24.590
Georgia could be a purple state that sometimes
votes for a Democrat.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:29.220
There are others you could actually end up
with a Democratic candidate with more than

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.120
400 electoral votes.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:33.070
But it will likely take some time.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:36.030
The Republican Party will fight it with gerrymandering.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:40.340
They will fight it with voter suppression
because they know they can't win on the issues.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.330
The other thing to consider is it's not in
the interests of corporate media to have a

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.960
blowout election.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:53.190
Imagine how many people would tune out if
in June all corporate media said this race

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:54.450
is over.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.319
And, of course, they would never actually
do that.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:03.490
But the corporate media has an interest to
keep it seeming as hard fought and close as

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:04.490
possible.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:07.010
So for the foreseeable future, what do we
know?

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.210
We know that elections will at least be portrayed
as close.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:16.889
And we also know that unless demographic changes
overwhelm the level of partizanship that we

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:20.660
have in this country, we are likely to see
contested alack.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:21.680
We will put it that way.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.920
Competitive elections for a very long time.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:25.920
OK.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:05:15.779
Last question today,

